First Author: Medved
Title: Improving prediction of heart transplantation outcome using deep learning techniques
Source number: 36  ￼

Very short summary: This UNOS registry study compared a deep-learning heart-transplant survival model (IHTSA) with the conventional IMPACT score for predicting post-transplant mortality. Five key findings were: (1) 27,860 adult heart transplants, corresponding to 27,705 patients, were analyzed; (2) one-year mortality was 13% (n = 3,561), with median survival 12 years; (3) in the 2009–2011 test cohort, recalibrated IHTSA achieved AUROC 0.65 versus 0.61 for IMPACT for 1-year mortality; (4) for long-term survival discrimination in the same test cohort, recalibrated IHTSA had C-index 0.63 versus 0.58 for IMPACT; and (5) calibration was better for IHTSA, with predicted 1-year mortality 12% versus actual 10%, compared with 22% for IMPACT. Tables 1–6 and Figures 1–3 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

